# tests/T19.bd - T19: throttle poll cost - does watching the
# meter hold it up?
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# PURPOSE
# throttle() (utils/net.bd:165) is exactly:
#     sleep(log_pace())            # log_pace() == 1
#     while get_cpu() > threshold:
#         sleep(log_pace())
# Its cost is the single most-cited number in this whole investigation:
# a near-constant ~3.7s per call, across ~10 samples spanning a 122s
# run, whether it followed a cheap 2.1s empty-field decode or an
# expensive 14.3s 3-item decode. That number has NEVER been broken down
# into "one mandatory sleep plus k retries" - the retry count has never
# once been counted. This test counts it, and then asks the question
# that number cannot answer on its own: is the poll loop a passive
# observer of a decaying meter, or does polling itself feed the load it
# is waiting on?
#
# THREE THINGS MEASURED, one run:
#   PHASE P (unpolled reference): after an identical load unit, sleep a
#     FIXED k seconds and take exactly ONE get_cpu() reading. k = 1..5.
#     The smallest k whose reading is <= 80 is how long the meter takes
#     to clear 80 WHEN NOBODY IS WATCHING IT. Nothing in this corpus has
#     ever measured that - every decay number we have was produced by a
#     loop that polled while it waited. This is the floor everything in
#     PHASE T is compared against.
#   PHASE T (polled sweep, 4 conditions interleaved per cycle):
#     iv=1/thr=80   - reproduces production throttle() exactly
#     iv=1/thr=50   - cpu_guard_conservative(); every throttle ever
#                     measured in this investigation used 80, never 50
#     iv=0.5/thr=80 - half interval, ~2x the polls per second
#     iv=0.25/thr=80- quarter interval, ~4x the polls per second
#   Conditions are INTERLEAVED (one of each, per cycle) rather than run
#   as separate executions, so session drift hits every arm equally.
#
# WHAT EACH POSSIBLE RESULT MEANS
#   polls= at iv=1/thr=80 is 3-5 and wait= is ~3.7s +/-0.5s
#     -> the famous constant is decomposed at last: 1 mandatory sleep +
#        (polls-1) retries. Every cost model in the corpus can finally
#        be written in terms of retries instead of a magic number.
#   polls= is 1 EVERY time and wait= is ~1.0s
#     -> the wait loop NEVER retries, and production's ~3.7s is NOT in
#        the wait loop at all - it is call/line overhead somewhere else.
#        That would retire "throttle costs 3.7s of CPU recovery" as a
#        misattribution and redirect the whole cost model. Pre-registered
#        as a live possibility, not a failed run.
#   wait= roughly FLAT across iv=1 / 0.5 / 0.25 while polls= scales
#   about 1:2:4
#     -> polling is passive; the wait is pure elapsed-time decay;
#        field_notes.txt's "polling more often does NOT help and can
#        make things WORSE" is wrong as stated. NOTE the asymmetry:
#        under pure decay the FASTER poller should finish ~0.4s EARLIER
#        on average (finer sampling grid), so "exactly equal" is already
#        weak evidence of a small perturbation, not of none.
#   wait= GROWS as iv shrinks (>2.0s from iv=1 to iv=0.25), or the
#   iv=0.25 arm hits its poll cap, or the script is killed in an
#   iv=0.25 round
#     -> the instrument sustains what it measures. throttle()'s ~3.7s is
#        then partly self-inflicted, the abandoned test_cpu_scale result
#        (6.7x slower at half the sleep) is reproduced at primitive
#        scale, and the three prior guess-and-kill attempts at
#        sub-second polling are explained rather than just survived.
#   PHASE P clears 80 materially FASTER than the iv=1 polled wait
#     -> the cleanest possible signature of the observer effect: the
#        meter drops sooner when it is not being read.
#   wait=(thr=50) - wait=(thr=80) > 1.0s at iv=1, and PHASE P crosses 80
#   and 50 at different k
#     -> the reading really is a decaying quantity through 50..80 and
#        cpu_guard_conservative() buys real headroom.
#   wait=(thr=50) and wait=(thr=80) within +/-0.3s, and PHASE P jumps
#   from >80 straight to <50 between adjacent k
#     -> a cliff, not a ramp. The reading does not decay smoothly through
#        that range, and cpu_guard_conservative()=50 has never bought
#        anything over cpu_guard_fast()=80 in the entire codebase.
#
# THE LOAD UNIT IS A DELIBERATE UNPACED BURST. Each round runs ~22
# unpaced lines (load_unit(5) plus 5 bookkeeping lines) between one
# sleep and the next. At base tier that is ~2.2s of execution - under
# the 5-second sustained-100% kill window by design, and IDENTICAL in
# every arm, so no wait= difference can be blamed on a different amount
# of preceding work. Do NOT raise loadn above ~10 without T11's
# measured kill boundary n* in hand: loadn=10 is already ~3.7s of
# unpaced execution, close to the window.
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   T19              # defaults: cycles=3 loadn=5, ~2.5-3 min
#   T19 5 5          # deeper, if cycle-to-cycle spread is big
#   T19 3 3          # lighter load, if the default arm is killed
# Run it on a quiet session (ps/jobs clear) and paste back EVERY line
# starting with "P ", "> " or "R ". A kill IS a result: the last "> "
# line printed names the exact iv/thr condition that killed it.
#
# REPEATS: one run gives 5 PHASE P points and 3 replicates per PHASE T
# condition, enough to see a 2s effect. Repeat the whole run once as a
# drift control. If the iv=0.25 arm is killed or caps out, repeat twice
# more to prove the kill is reproducible rather than phase luck.
#
# @param cycles "how many times to repeat the 4-condition cycle" default=3
# @param loadn "iterations in the fixed load unit (about half T11's n*)" default=5

func load_unit(n):
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    while j < n:
        x = x + j
        j = j + 1
    return x

var T0 = get_unix_time()
log("T19 launch cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
var CYCLES = int(get_param("cycles", "3").replace("\"", "").trim())
var LOADN = int(get_param("loadn", "5").replace("\"", "").trim())
var SETTLE = 2
sleep(1)
log("T19 start cycles=" + str(CYCLES) + " loadn=" + str(LOADN) + " settle=" + str(SETTLE))
sleep(1)
log("--- PHASE P: unpolled decay, exactly 1 get_cpu() per round ---")

for k in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]:
    sleep(SETTLE)
    var ppre = get_cpu()
    var pls = get_unix_time()
    var pxx = load_unit(LOADN)
    var plb = get_unix_time() - pls
    sleep(k)
    var pc = get_cpu()
    sleep(1)
    log("P k=" + str(k) + " pre=" + str(ppre) + " load=" + str(plb) + " after=" + str(pc))

sleep(2)
log("--- PHASE T: polled throttle sweep, 4 conditions per cycle ---")
var IVS = [1, 1, 0.5, 0.25]
var THRS = [80, 50, 80, 80]
var CAPS = [12, 12, 24, 48]
sleep(1)

var cyc = 1
while cyc <= CYCLES:
    for k in [0, 1, 2, 3]:
        var iv = IVS[k]
        var thr = THRS[k]
        var cap = CAPS[k]
        log("> c" + str(cyc) + " iv=" + str(iv) + " thr=" + str(thr))
        sleep(SETTLE)
        var pre = get_cpu()
        var ls = get_unix_time()
        var xx = load_unit(LOADN)
        var lb = get_unix_time() - ls
        var ws = get_unix_time()
        sleep(iv)
        var n = 1
        var c = get_cpu()
        while c > thr and n < cap:
            sleep(iv)
            n = n + 1
            c = get_cpu()
        var el = get_unix_time() - ws
        sleep(1)
        log("R c" + str(cyc) + " iv=" + str(iv) + " thr=" + str(thr) + " pre=" + str(pre) + " load=" + str(lb) + " wait=" + str(el) + " polls=" + str(n) + "/" + str(cap) + " exit=" + str(c))
    cyc = cyc + 1

sleep(1)
log("T19 done total=" + str(get_unix_time() - T0) + "s - paste every P / > / R line")
